docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/blending.mdx:40:In an HDR (HLG) project, "linear" means HLG's scene light.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/dynamic-range.mdx:58:SDR material is lifted so its white sits at HDR reference white. It looks as it did, and HDR highlights rise above it.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/dynamic-range.mdx:62:White is fixed at 203 cd/m² (HLG 75 %). There's no setting for the reference white.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/dynamic-range.mdx:66:Everything Frame Job draws is drawn as it is in SDR, then lifted to HDR reference white. That means text, captions, burn-in timecode, shapes, SDR stills, graphics, and the background and shadow colours.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/dynamic-range.mdx:68:So a white title sits at reference white, not in the highlights, and a white graphic matches SDR white. Text, captions, shapes and graphics stay 8-bit even though they're lifted. See [Precision and bit depth](/hdr/precision/).
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/dynamic-range.mdx:72:Switching changes no grade value. The numbers stay as you set them, and the grade then runs on the project's working colour, with BT.2020's weights in an HDR (HLG) project.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/export-hdr.mdx:61:The project's SDR version, in Rec. 709. It's the same picture as [Show SDR Version](/hdr/show-sdr-version/). White titles stay near white, and highlights above reference white are rolled off.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/hdr-scopes.mdx:8:In an HDR (HLG) project the scopes measure the HLG signal instead of Rec. 709, and their scales and targets change to match. Use them to see how far a highlight rises above white, and to check that titles and SDR clips sit at reference white.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/hdr-scopes.mdx:38:The dashed 75 % line is HDR reference white, and only an HDR (HLG) signal has it. Titles and SDR material sit on it, because they're [lifted to reference white](/hdr/dynamic-range/). HDR highlights rise above it.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/hdr-scopes.mdx:63:`render_frame` measures the same four scopes and gives an agent their numbers. In an HDR (HLG) project those numbers say they're of the HLG signal. The Waveform, Parade and Histogram also carry the scale: each line in cd/m², with reference white at 75 %. A frame asked for as the SDR version gets an SDR project's scopes, with nothing added. See [Check the picture: render_frame](/mcp/render-frame/).
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/hdr10-peak.mdx:29:- **In an SDR project**, highlights roll off smoothly to white at the peak. A peak below 203 cd/m², HDR reference white, counts as 203.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/hdr10-peak.mdx:30:- **In an HDR (HLG) project**, the picture keeps display light up to 1,000 cd/m². A peak above that rolls brighter highlights off to fit, and a lower peak doesn't change the picture.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/log-luts.mdx:75:- In an HDR (HLG) project, a LUT's output is treated as SDR and [lifted to reference white](/hdr/dynamic-range/#sdr-material-in-an-hdr-project), so the file gains no HDR highlights. A built-in curve does become real HDR there.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/show-sdr-version.mdx:46:- The picture comes back as it was, up to a little below HDR reference white.
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/show-sdr-version.mdx:48:- Highlights above reference white are rolled off, and reach full white at 1,000 cd/m².
docs/site/src/content/docs/hdr/precision.mdx:47:Text, shapes, captions, graphics and screen recordings are 8-bit, and so are thumbnails. In an HDR (HLG) project those layers are still [lifted to HDR reference white](/hdr/dynamic-range/), so they sit with the rest of the picture.
